Hello everyone, I’m Nishanth, and I’m joining the DS&AI team as an Associate Director. This is actually my third chapter at Novartis. My first was with Conext, my second with NBS IT, and now I’m back for version 3.0 with DS&AI. One relationship has remained consistent across all three versions: Arun has been my manager. So, if I represented my career as a knowledge graph, that would probably be its most stable edge!

On the personal side, I’m based in Hyderabad and have two young sons. At work, I spend my time training models. At home, my children make sure I continue receiving training myself, usually through real-time feedback and very little documentation. As for hobbies, I currently classify them as an endangered species. The interest is still there; the available time is usually null.

I graduated from NIT Rourkela in 2016 with an integrated B.Tech and M.Tech in Statistical Modelling in Water Resources. That background gave me an appreciation for uncertainty, mathematical modelling and understanding how systems behave. Over roughly ten years, I’ve applied those foundations to problems across healthcare and retail. What has kept me interested is seeing an idea become something people can actually use in their everyday work.

My experience includes Walmart and Optum, and I’ll focus on those chapters to give you a sense of my technical interests. At Walmart, I was part of the Retail Graph and Trust & Safety teams. These brought together two areas that have become central to my work: learning from relationships and combining information across different modalities.

The Retail Graph provided a connected view of entities such as stores, sellers and items. Rather than beginning with individual projects, I like to explain the foundation first. We had entities, their attributes and the relationships between them. A graph database helped us store and retrieve that connected information, while graph queries allowed us to explore relationships relevant to different business questions.

On top of that foundation, we built several kinds of intelligence. Node similarity helped identify comparable stores, sellers or items. Community detection helped surface groups with shared characteristics, including potential seller collusion. These approaches supported applications across catalogue quality, recommendations, search and forecasting. I found this particularly interesting because the same underlying relationships could become useful in several different ways.

We also used graph neural networks to learn from both entity attributes and their surrounding connections. One application was seller fraud detection, where looking at a seller in isolation could miss signals visible in the broader network. Another was forecasting demand for cold-start situations, such as an item entering a new store. Temporal graph approaches helped bring relationships and historical behaviour into the modelling process.

Another application involved duplicate-item detection. Product listings can describe essentially the same item using different wording, images or attributes. Combining extracted entities with knowledge graphs, similarity methods and community detection helped us identify related listings and investigate potential duplicates. Across these problems, my interest was in choosing the representation that made the underlying business problem easier to solve.

The other major part of my Walmart work was multimodal catalogue intelligence and Trust & Safety. Product information rarely arrives in one clean format. Important evidence may appear in the title, description, image or brand-related attributes. Combining those signals helped us address problems such as counterfeit detection, offensive-content detection and catalogue attribute extraction.

This work involved vision models, vision-language models and language models. My focus also extended to fine-tuning and post-training smaller and larger models, including supervised fine-tuning, DPO and approaches using AI feedback. For counterfeit and offensive-content detection, the goal was to make model behaviour better aligned with the task and the distinctions that mattered in practice.

I enjoy the decisions around that process: how to construct useful training examples, identify difficult negatives, evaluate errors and decide whether an improvement justifies its production cost. A model can look impressive on a few examples and still struggle with the cases that matter most. That makes evaluation, data quality and understanding failure modes as important to me as the modelling technique itself.

At Optum, my work included product approval automation and data interoperability. One area involved transferring capabilities from larger language models into smaller models through knowledge distillation, supporting product tagging and categorisation. This continued a theme from my earlier work: adapting model capabilities to a specific operational problem while considering efficiency and scale.

The interoperability work brought graphs and language models together in another setting. Here, the connected information included code, business rules, transformation logic and tests. Separate graph views could connect through shared entities and dependencies, allowing GraphRAG to retrieve relevant context. That context could support code generation, human-guided changes, change-impact analysis and root-cause investigation.

What interests me about this approach is the connection between intent and implementation. A business rule describes what should happen, code implements it, and tests check expected behaviour. Connecting those pieces makes it easier to reason about a change and understand what else it might affect. Human feedback and validation remain important parts of that workflow.

If I were to summarise my expertise visually, I would describe it as having two main peaks: Graph AI and language-model post-training. Around those, I have broader experience in multimodal AI, classical machine learning, optimisation and agentic systems. I enjoy combining these areas when the problem calls for it, while keeping the solution understandable and grounded in evidence.

Beyond the technical work, I value collaboration with people who bring different perspectives. I’ve worked closely with engineering, data and business teams to take ideas into production. I enjoy discussing modelling choices, learning from colleagues and helping others work through difficult problems. Those conversations often improve the solution as much as another experiment does.

Coming back to Novartis, I’m looking forward to bringing these experiences into the team, reconnecting with familiar colleagues and building new relationships. There is plenty for me to learn, and I’m excited to contribute to work that makes a meaningful difference. Thank you, and I look forward to working with all of you.
